<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<model version="NetLogo 7.0.4" snapToGrid="false">
  <code><![CDATA[extensions [gis resource]

globals [states-dataset airports-dataset]

breed [airports airport]
airports-own [ abbrev name population infected-population]
breed [planes plane]
planes-own [ destination infected ]

to load-datasets
  clear-all
  set states-dataset gis:load-dataset-from-string "shp" resource:get "states_continental"
  set airports-dataset gis:load-dataset-from-string "shp" resource:get "airports_with_population_of_nearest_city"
end

to custom-clear
  clear-turtles
  clear-patches
  clear-drawing
  clear-all-plots
  ;; reset any other global variables you might be using here
end

to setup
  custom-clear

  ;; loading datasets can take time, so only do it on the first SETUP
  if states-dataset = 0 or airports-dataset = 0 [
    load-datasets
  ]

  ask patches [ set pcolor blue - 3 ]
  gis:set-world-envelope gis:envelope-of states-dataset

  gis:set-drawing-color yellow
  gis:draw states-dataset 1

  foreach gis:feature-list-of airports-dataset [ this-airport-vector-feature ->
    let location gis:location-of gis:centroid-of this-airport-vector-feature
    create-airports 1 [
      set xcor item 0 location
      set ycor item 1 location
      set population gis:property-value this-airport-vector-feature "pop_max"
      set abbrev gis:property-value this-airport-vector-feature "abbrev"
      set name gis:property-value this-airport-vector-feature "name"
      set label abbrev
      set shape "circle"
      set infected-population 0
      set color black
      set size sqrt (population / 500000)
    ]
  ]

  ask airports [
    create-links-with up-to-n-of 3 other airports in-radius 30
  ]

  ask one-of airports [
    set infected-population 1
  ]
  reset-ticks
end

to go
  ask airports [
    calculate-infections
    recolor-airport
    hatch-departing-planes
  ]
  ask planes [ fly ]
  tick
end

to calculate-infections
  set infected-population infected-population + growth * infected-population * (1 - (infected-population / population))
end

to recolor-airport
  set color scale-color red (infected-population / population) 0 2
end

to hatch-departing-planes
  let possible-destinations link-neighbors
  hatch-planes random 2 [
    set label ""
    set size 1
    set color white
    set shape "airplane"
    set infected false

    set destination one-of possible-destinations
    face destination
    if random-float 1 < ([infected-population] of myself / [population] of myself) [
      set color red
      set infected true
    ]
  ]
end

to fly
    if distance destination < 1 [
      if infected [
        ask destination [
         set infected-population infected-population + 1
       ]
      ]
      die
    ]
    fd 1
end


; Public Domain:
; To the extent possible under law, Uri Wilensky has waived all
; copyright and related or neighboring rights to this model.]]></code>
  <widgets>
    <view x="210" wrappingAllowedX="false" y="10" frameRate="30.0" minPycor="-16" height="334" showTickCounter="true" patchSize="10.0" fontSize="10" wrappingAllowedY="false" width="654" tickCounterLabel="ticks" maxPycor="16" updateMode="1" maxPxcor="32" minPxcor="-32"></view>
    <button x="67" y="95" height="40" disableUntilTicks="false" forever="false" kind="Observer" width="66">setup</button>
    <button x="68" y="143" height="40" disableUntilTicks="true" forever="true" kind="Observer" width="63">go</button>
    <input x="30" multiline="false" y="197" height="60" variable="growth" type="number" width="149">0.1</input>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## WHAT IS IT?

This model was built to demonstrate the vector (feature) functionality of the GIS NetLogo extension. It loads the locations of major world airports along with the outlines of the continental United States, builds a flight network among nearby airports, and simulates an infection spreading from airport to airport as planes travel between them.

## HOW IT WORKS

The model loads two vector datasets: a polygon dataset of state outlines and a point dataset of airports. For each airport feature, it reads that feature's location and properties (its name, abbreviation, and the population of the nearest city) and creates a turtle at the corresponding spot on the map, sizing it according to that population.

Each airport is then linked to a few nearby airports, forming a flight network, and one airport begins with a small infected population. On each tick, every airport's infected population grows logistically, and airports hatch planes bound for neighboring airports. A plane is infected with a probability proportional to how infected its origin airport is; when an infected plane reaches its destination, it raises the infected population there. Over time, the infection spreads across the network, and airports are colored from black to red to show how infected they are.

## HOW TO USE IT

Press the SETUP button, which on first press calls `load-datasets` to put the GIS data into global variables.  Once the data is loaded it will draw the map, place the airports, and build the flight network. Press GO to run the simulation.

The GROWTH input controls how quickly the infected population grows at each airport.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

Notice that the airports are sized by the population of their nearest city, so the busiest hubs stand out. Watch how the infection tends to reach large, well-connected hubs and then spread outward from them.

## THINGS TO TRY

Try changing the GROWTH value and observe how it affects the speed at which the infection takes hold across the network.

## EXTENDING THE MODEL

The flight network is currently built by linking each airport to a few others within a fixed radius. Try using the airports' population data to build a more realistic network in which busier airports have more connections. You could also let planes carry infection between airports that are not directly linked.

## NETLOGO FEATURES

This model uses the `gis` extension to load vector datasets from bundled resources with `gis:load-dataset-from-string`, to draw them, and to read individual features with `gis:feature-list-of`, `gis:centroid-of`, `gis:location-of`, and `gis:property-value`.

## RELATED MODELS

The other GIS code examples, GIS General Examples and GIS Gradient Example, provide additional examples of how to use the GIS extension.

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

This model is based on the [GIS extension vector tutorial](https://github.com/NetLogo/GIS-Extension/wiki/Vector-Tutorial).

<!-- 2025 -->]]></info>
